// `out` and `ref` lower to the same `T*`, so the marker is what tells a reader (and the caller's
// definite-assignment analysis) that the variable is being FILLED rather than borrowed. Omitting it is
// how `out` silently degenerated into an alias for `ref`; it is now a hard error.
fn void fill(out int32 dst) { dst = 7; }
fn int main() {
    int32 x = 0;
    fill(dst: x);     // ERROR: must say `dst: out x`
    return x;
}
